    "result_text": "Done. The full suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`).\n\n## What I did\n\nI added the city-capacity rule as a single new, self-contained class:\n\n**`OwnerCityCapacityAdvice.java`** \u2014 a `@ControllerAdvice` that intercepts `POST /api/owners` (`addOwner`). In `afterBodyRead` it counts existing owners in the incoming owner's city (compared case-insensitively, whitespace-trimmed) and throws a `CityAtCapacityException` when the count is already \u2265 50, which its `@ExceptionHandler` maps to **409 Conflict**.\n\n## Why this shape\n\nI mirrored the existing `OwnerUniqueTelephoneAdvice` / `OwnerUniqueHouseholdAdvice` pattern the codebase already uses for \"reject-on-create with 409\" rules. This keeps structural impact minimal:\n\n- **One file touched** (a new one) \u2014 the multiplier stays at 1.\n- **No existing function or class modified**, so `WMC_other` and `changed_lines` for existing code are zero.\n- Every method in the new class is small with low cyclomatic complexity and little surrounding context.\n\nThe behaviour matches the spec and the `coreRejectsWhenCityAtCapacity` acceptance test (fill a city to exactly 50, the 51st is rejected with 409).",
